## Solution

We seek the property of a measure space $(X, \Sigma, \mu)$ guaranteeing $L_\infty(\mu) \subseteq L_p(\mu)$ for every $p \geq 1$.

**Claim.** The required property is that the measure space is **finite**, i.e., $\mu(X) < \infty$.

### Proof

**Sufficiency.** Suppose $\mu(X) < \infty$. Let $f \in L_\infty(\mu)$, so $\|f\|_\infty = \operatorname{ess\,sup}|f| < \infty$. For any $p \geq 1$,

$$\|f\|_p^p = \int_X |f|^p\,d\mu \leq \|f\|_\infty^p \cdot \mu(X) < \infty,$$

since $|f(x)| \leq \|f\|_\infty$ almost everywhere, and $\mu(X)$ is finite. Hence $f \in L_p(\mu)$ for all $p \geq 1$.

**Necessity.** Suppose $\mu(X) = \infty$. Consider the constant function $f \equiv 1$. Then $f \in L_\infty(\mu)$ with $\|f\|_\infty = 1$. However,

$$\|f\|_p^p = \int_X 1\,d\mu = \mu(X) = \infty,$$

so $f \notin L_p(\mu)$ for any $p \geq 1$. Thus $L_\infty(\mu) \not\subseteq L_p(\mu)$.

**Conclusion.** The property ensuring $L_\infty(\mu) \subseteq L_p(\mu)$ for all $p \geq 1$ is precisely that the measure space is **finite**: $\mu(X) < \infty$.

